    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**1. `Owner.java` \u2014 the membership logic** (`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`)\n- Added `hasLargeHousehold(Integer householdMemberCount)` \u2014 the new \"household of 3 or more\" factor, following the same static-helper pattern as the existing `hasEmail` / `hasNoNamesakes` / `hasLongTenure` factors.\n- Added `getMembershipPoints()`: starts at `0`, `+2` email present, `+1` namesakeCount 0, `+2` household of 3+, `+3` tenure over 365 days.\n- Rewrote `getMembershipLevel()` to derive purely from points: `1` (0\u20131), `2` (2\u20133), `3` (4\u20135), `4` (6+). This replaces the old rule that computed the level directly.\n\n**2. `openapi.yml`** (`src/main/resources/`)\n- Added the `membershipPoints` schema property (readOnly, min 0).\n- Updated `membershipLevel`'s description and raised its `maximum` from `3` to `4` to match the new mapping.\n\nThe MapStruct `OwnerMapper` maps both fields automatically by name (as it already did for `membershipLevel`), and `householdMemberCount` is populated by the controller before mapping, so no changes were needed there.\n\nThe acceptance test's scenario checks out: a fresh owner with an email (+2) and no namesake (+1), single-member household (+0), zero tenure (+0) \u2192 **3 points \u2192 level 2**, exactly as asserted.",
